{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1480924-bone-gnawers","id":1480924,"title":"Bone gnawers","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-05-12T21:27:16-07:00","id":1480924,"title":"Bone gnawers","content":"I have spent almost a year reading the tribe books and the kith books and now I am coming to an end, having only one more to read. The last one I have read is the bone gnawer revised tribe book, and I really like this tribe! Probably, one of the the top 3 tribes which I like and one the bests tribe books written, in my opinion.<br /> <br /> The only thing that bored me to exhaustion was the USA patriotism of the text, there are other countries out there, emerged and built under the idea of freedom and opportunities. And, besides, the outcast have existed since the beginning of times, I see no reason why they are more numerous in USA.<br /> <br /> Anyway, I liked the book, it is plenty of cool character ideas, it is remarkable different in both, cultures and aesthetics, and it begins with an interesting short story. The totems and spirits are also useful, although perhaps the level 1 gifts are uninspired, being reduced to dirty combate style (explosives and stones) and ways of make some easy money. The premade characters are all playable and I have not found any racial issues or political troubles in the text, which seems to be a constant in werewolf.<br /> <br /> So, what do you like about the Bone gnawers and what would you change? Would you change the motto to &quot;anything for a chessburger&quot;? Do you like allying them with the ratkin? It is USA the land of the homeless?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24202-justycar","name":"Justycar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24202_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"320"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-12T23:50:10-07:00","id":1480940,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Justycar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1480924-bone-gnawers#post1480924\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The only thing that bored me to exhaustion was the USA patriotism of the text, there are other countries out there, emerged and built under the idea of freedom and opportunities. And, besides, the outcast have existed since the beginning of times, I see no reason why they are more numerous in USA.</div> </div> </div> </div>While the WoD found a worldwide fanbase, White Wolf was an American company primarily catering to an American fanbase, and its sourcebooks and other gaming material reflects this.<br /> <br /> Although I am an American, I have always been a world history buff and often found its depiction of history to be too centered on America. It's certainly OK to depict &quot;this is how the tribe is in the USA&quot; as long as you acknowledge the tribe is present in other areas and has according differences. As such, we can understand the Bone Gnawers in the favelas of Brazil, slums of India, and so one would be different in ways that make sense. I'd encourage people who live and ST in different areas to adjust as necessary so the tribe makes sense there. (Or indeed any other tribe).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Justycar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1480924-bone-gnawers#post1480924\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, what do you like about the Bone gnawers and what would you change? Would you change the motto to &quot;anything for a chessburger&quot;? Do you like allying them with the ratkin? It is USA the land of the homeless?</div> </div> </div> </div>I can honestly state that while I do like the tribe in many ways, I think fundamentally the tribe is depicted wrongly in official materials. The key to the tribe is that they are the &quot;omega tribe&quot;. They are the &quot;runts of the litter&quot; of the Garou Nation. That does not necessarily mean &quot;homeless bums&quot;. There's no reason it shouldn't include those. But it should not be definitive. So while many canonical depictions are appropriate, the developers and writers often got it wrong to my taste. What the &quot;omega tribe&quot; means is going to be different in many different human cultures.<br /> <br /> And I think the Bone Gnawers are often depicted wrong in the USA because the writers fundamentally did not understand the reality of homelessness in America. There are two types of homeless. The first are people who are down on the luck and need a temporary helping hand. Those kind of people - people who are capable of holding down a job and living on their own - are typically only homeless for about 6 months at most. Then they find a job, save up some money, and are able to move into an apartment again. Various charities and homeless shelters help them in the meantime. Of course, that time of difficulty can be adjusted on various economic factors. The second type of homeless are people who are either mentally ill or long term drug addicts. Their problems are fundamentally different than the first kind, and require very different solutions. Unfortunately, many advocates of the homeless pretend these second kind either do not exist or promote solutions completely inappropriate to them. There is an extreme problem of &quot;pathological altruism&quot; where the &quot;solutions&quot; do not actually address the real root causes. And the material on the Bone Gnawers effectively pretends this second group does not exist. This is very different than the underclass situation in other countries where true lack of economic opportunity exists for many people.<br /> <br /> So in my chronicles, I try to keep to the essentials of how the tribe should be (the omega tribe, the runts of the Garou Nation, socially disadvantageous, and a combination of outraged social justice combined with severe problems that undermine both the tribe and Garou Nation) and change a lot of the specifics to reflect my view of reality.<br /> <br /> My advice to everyone is to do the same. Figure out the essentials and adapt as necessary for your chronicle. We'll all have different interpretations. That's OK.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-13T00:10:00-07:00","id":1480942,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1480924-bone-gnawers?p=1480940#post1480940\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> And the material on the Bone Gnawers effectively pretends this second group does not exist. This is very different than the underclass situation in other countries where true lack of economic opportunity exists for many people.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>I'd actually say that it is the reverse. The books go on and on about how most on the street are mentally ill and unable to keep a job, with there being a (very penalizing) merit if you even have a regular address.<br /> <br /> Those people who are just temporary homeless are not represented much in the tribe. The assumption is that you are stuck on the streets or mooching off your friends.<br /> <br /> In general, WtA has kind of an issue equating social standing among the garou with social standing among humans. This might work for Silver Fangs, but since most garou cannot hold jobs and are reliant on their kinfolk it makes no sense the Gnawers are the only ones who are portrayed as homeless.<br /> <br /> Personally, I've tried to include the highly educated but poor people among the tribe's members and kin. Or those who do not like the idea of the rank system.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://ana-mizuki.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My gallery.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/110-ana-mizuki","name":"Ana Mizuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar110_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2484"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-13T07:27:50-07:00","id":1480962,"title":"","content":"I understand the focus in USA in the books, the By nights, the plots, the characters, the geography chapters in the tribebooks. However, I was talking about the patriotism and USA-centrism of the tribe (I refuse using &quot;Am\u00e9rica&quot; for a single country in the whole continent). Almost it seems that USA invented the freedom and the homelessness, it could even be a parody, I do not know.<br /> <br /> Anyway, the members of the tribe depicted in that book are permanent homeless without the will or the capacity to leave this kind of life. In the bone gnawers we found more &quot;lunatics&quot; than in the other tribes, garou with mental illnesses, alcoholics, and the detritus of the garou nation, in general. They mention other kind of BG though, the rural redneck who lives isolated in the Appalachians or the Bayou, some of them even cannibalistic.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24202-justycar","name":"Justycar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24202_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"320"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-13T14:45:38-07:00","id":1481011,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ana Mizuki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1480924-bone-gnawers?p=1480942#post1480942\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Those people who are just temporary homeless are not represented much in the tribe. The assumption is that you are stuck on the streets or mooching off your friends.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think the tribe isn't always presented coherently or consistently. It's all over the place. That's not necessarily a problem. Lots of inspiration can be good. I just don't think it's tied well together. From the corebook you get the impression that it is all hobos and street bums. But the tribebooks introduce a lot of camps which really expand the tribe. And while they don't match the corebook description, do fit the idea that the tribe is among the low income.<br /> <br /> The Frankweilers don't seem like severely mentally ill people unable to exercise control over their own lives. The camp description talks about educating people, helping them improve lives, and even lawyers are members of it. They can still be relatively low socio-economic class, but we are very far from severely mentally ill people and drug street people.<br /> <br /> Likewise, it's hard to imagine the Rat Finks being so dysfunctional. They're supposed to be running one of the best intelligence networks in the World of Darkness. And when talking about occupations that they (or more accurately, their kinfolk) are having include janitors, trash collectors, waiters, clerks, receptionists, etc. These are all fairly low status jobs and often don't earn much money. But these are not people who can't find places to live, or obviously unable to care for themselves.<br /> <br /> The Hood are supposed to be criminals that steal from the rich and give to the poor. They don't seem to organized crimelords. They're more like street gangs engaged in local protection rackets and targeted theft who then provide help to the destitute. This doesn't sound like homeless people. They have enough cash to give it to others. They probably make pretty good money. Nothing that would make them obscenely rich, but wealthy and powerful enough that they can push out loan sharks and rival street gangs that would otherwise infest their domain.<br /> <br /> The Deserters spend most of their time in the Umbra so discussion on their socioeconomic status is besides the point. But clearly umbral exploration takes a lot of skill.<br /> <br /> Hillfolk live in isolated mountain homes often cut off from the outside area so they're not doing well. But they eke out a living on their own in rural poverty or low income, and aren't dependent on others.<br /> <br /> Sure, you have the unnamed typical Bone Gnawer of the corebook, the Man Eaters, and the Rabble. But for the most part they aren't expanded upon. Maybe it's just my bad memory, but I don't remember much discussion that the Bone Gnawers themselves are mentally ill or drug addicts. Although those are acceptable character concepts that would fit in, and certainly some NPCs match that. The Man Eaters tribe can certainly be seen as an analogue to drug addicts living on the streets.<br /> <br /> And I have to admit I have never yet seen a PC who is an actual homeless Bone Gnawer. They're poor, yes. But they're usually very competent, just doing dirty jobs and providing services to the community. And I almost never see NPCs in chronicles that show any tremendous dysfunction among the Bone Gnawers.<br /> <br /> But we can get a lot of Merits, Flaws, and Tribal Disadvantages that seem to support this is what they are. Like no Resources ever. They're permanently without money of any kind. Where are the janitors, night school trained lawyers, waiters, garbagemen, and others who seem to earn a living? We have this strange dichotomy within the tribe. On one hand are really functional Bone Gnawers that don't seem well supported by the rules. Or homeless street bums that don't match many actual characters, but seem to be permanently homeless. Few characters in between that show much more social mobility. <br /> <br /> Nor do we get much direction on what happens should a Bone Gnawer Garou or kinfolk becomes too successful. Do they just migrate out of the tribe and be accepted elsewhere? Who would accept these ex-BG &quot;refugees&quot;? Maybe Glass Walkers and Children of Gaia, but what about others? Does Rat abandon the Garou, or have some unmentioned tribal ban? If not, what's to stop some successful kinfolk or Garou expanding their career or business and acting as a real benefactor to the rest of the local tribe? Is there social pressure to prevent this - that such Garou or kinfolk are getting too big for their britches and need to be less ambitious? Or are such characters plucked up by one or more of the other tribes and the Gnawers can't do anything about it?<br /> <br /> This is what I am talking about. The Bone Gnawers seem to be portrayed as perpetually poor and never able to move up. But that's not realistic, even if most of the tribe may not. And some of the camps seem to sit uneasily given other things we're told. If the Hood or Frankweilers can help some people (including kinfolk) get off the street and move up to hold a steady job, maybe even get some education and get a better job, then that could continue. It makes sense that it would happen, but the books don't see to address this possibility despite setting it up for us. What happens for those who continue to progress? One doesn't need to pretend that is what happens for everyone to address it. Instead we seem to have this weird situation of two levels of BG society (the classic homeless street bum of the corebook, and the elevated working poor of many camps) that are in stasis where nobody moves from their tier to the next, and certainly never progresses from that?<br /> <br /> So it doesn't seem articulated well to me. I have lots of questions that the books don't answer. So I had to answer themselves as best I can while trying to remain true to the &quot;spirit&quot; of the tribe. Maybe you don't have the same problems with the material that I do, or see the tension, or interpret it from a different angle. Which is fine. Everyone has their own ideas and takeaways.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-13T15:19:28-07:00","id":1481014,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1480924-bone-gnawers?p=1481011#post1481011\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But we can get a lot of Merits, Flaws, and Tribal Disadvantages that seem to support this is what they are. Like no Resources ever. They're permanently without money of any kind. Where are the janitors, night school trained lawyers, waiters, garbagemen, and others who seem to earn a living? We have this strange dichotomy within the tribe. On one hand are really functional Bone Gnawers that don't seem well supported by the rules. Or homeless street bums that don't match many actual characters, but seem to be permanently homeless. Few characters in between that show much more social mobility.</div> </div> </div> </div>From what I got from revised tribebook, in the section talking about backgrounds for the characters, Bone Gnawers are apparently INCAPABLE of holding onto money. The tribebook out and out says that the Gnawer will splurge it all and they will eternally annoy their packmates enough to get kicked back to the streets.<br /> <br /> I fully agree with you that this makes no sense, especially as characters of other tribes can have resources 0 too. Yet a Get of Fenris Ahroun is expected to have a home and have cool stuff, while a Bone Gnawer Ragabash is stuck on the streets. Basically, the omega status is artificially added to the character, rather than through any tribal culture.<br /> <br /> My solution to this was to turn the inability to gain money into a choice to avoid getting trapped in the Weaver's webs. Essentially, Gnawers intentionally live on the streets and intentionally live without many resources. This is to live 'wild' in the streets where Glass Walkers won't go.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://ana-mizuki.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My gallery.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/110-ana-mizuki","name":"Ana Mizuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar110_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2484"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-13T19:16:59-07:00","id":1481034,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ana Mizuki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1480924-bone-gnawers?p=1481014#post1481014\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">From what I got from revised tribebook, in the section talking about backgrounds for the characters, Bone Gnawers are apparently INCAPABLE of holding onto money. The tribebook out and out says that the Gnawer will splurge it all and they will eternally annoy their packmates enough to get kicked back to the streets.<br /> <br /> I fully agree with you that this makes no sense</div> </div> </div> </div>Yep, that is just bad world building. Every single Bone Gnawer in world history is incapable of keeping any kind of wealth? And their kinfolk too? Does the term &quot;agency&quot; mean anything tot he writers?<br /> <br /> That would be fine if they said that was part of Bone Gnawer culture and MOST characters (meaning NPCs since PCs will always choose to do what they want) are spendthrifts. Saving money is hard to do as many people don't have the self control that is necessary. It's easy to piss away money. But it's just not realistic to say every Bone Gnawer is incapable of that.<br /> <br /> They could have gone down the route that some kind of totem ban was involved. Raven for example explicitly doesn't want its pack children to carry wealth but trust in him to provide. Something similar could have explained a lot. Another route was to create something specific in the tribal culture, such as the expectation that any Bone Gnawer or kinfolk family with money is expected to provide for the others at the sept or protectorate. Money quickly goes away when individuals or families can't keep the money they earn themselves, but have to share it with their clan relatives or communities. Maybe that's part of providing &quot;loyal service&quot; to tribe or sept so at least they can get some Renown while getting screwed over financially. Sharing among everyone is an excellent survival strategy when living in some peasant village for most of humanity's history. But it prevents wealth accumulation in a modern economy. That would make some good tension within the tribe, and explain why it remains poor. There can be negative aspects to too much egalitarianism, but if Rat values the Swarm above the individual rat, it would make sense that this is an important part of tribal culture.<br /> <br /> But I think the tribe and setting loses some interesting conflict by not having certain Bone Gnawers being able to buck that system. They piss off Rat by not following the chiminage of the ban and keeping money, or anger the other kin at the sept by holding onto their money. Of course, now you have to explain what happens. Does the tribe perform the Rite of the Lone Wolf on them and kick them out? Do they become Ronin, or perhaps get accepted by one of the other tribes? How does the tribe treat the &quot;traitors&quot; of Rat who abandoned their responsibilities to their kind by becoming &quot;greedy&quot;? The idea of a Stone of Scorn in Central Park, with the names of traitorous former Bone Gnawers whose crime was putting too much money in a savings account, is something I'd perversely enjoy. It would simultaneously show the good and bad sides of Bone Gnawer culture.<br /> <br /> We'd also have another source of conflict within the game. Tensions possibly within the tribe and kinfolk itself, and possibly intertribal tensions as the tribe holds grudges against former tribe members who are now either Ronin or members of one of the other tribes. I personally like conflict like this in Werewolf because I feel too much of the game gets wrapped into the struggle with the Wyrm. That's welcome too, but has a tendency to suck the air out of all other kinds of interesting conflicts. It's like if the only acceptable conflict in Vampire is for Camarilla to fight Sabbat and vice versa (though Gaia knows Justin Achilli has tried his best to make that so).<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-13T23:36:43-07:00","id":1481050,"title":"","content":"If I understood of the book, the more heroic and successful young Bone Gnawers are sometimes adopted by other tribes, so the way is bidirectional, the BG accept the worst of all the tribes and they send back some promises to them. There is a merit of 1 point in the page 79 that equates to a point in resources (Struggling), the characters with this merit still maintain miserable jobs and achieve to pay a rent, but most of the tribe lives in the street without this benefit.<br /> <br /> About Rat and other totems, I think that the Rat accepts you as you are. It is said in the book that many BG of the rabble are barely functional werewolves, they live in the rural areas, appear once a month (if they do) in the caern and many have mental illnesses and other problems. But the BG hope that when the moment arrives, they answer. It is a point of conflict, one of the main miseries of the tribe, problems they are handling (page 59).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24202-justycar","name":"Justycar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24202_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"320"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-14T02:11:14-07:00","id":1481054,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1480924-bone-gnawers?p=1481034#post1481034\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Yep, that is just bad world building. Every single Bone Gnawer in world history is incapable of keeping any kind of wealth? And their kinfolk too? Does the term &quot;agency&quot; mean anything tot he writers?<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>I actually think the agency part is the main issue. Bone Gnawers by their concept (the omega wolves, poor, homeless, runts of the litter, etc.) lack agency. While Yellowstone wolf packs (which are WEIRD) have alpha wolves, the omega wolf is entirely a human-created situation. If a wolf does not fit in the pack in the wild, it will leave. But in zoos, they cannot leave and so they must deal with other wolves bullying them. Homeless are homeless often due to situations beyond their control (i.e. throwing the mentally ill to the streets, gentrification) as are the poor.<br /> <br /> By their concept, the Gnawers are more of a dumping ground of garou than really a tribe.<br /> <br /> However, this makes little sense if you look at the setting as a whole. For one, the Children of Gaia exist to take in those unfortunate. Second, as I said in my previous post, most garou are moochers by necessity. There is nothing really stopping a Gnawer from using their skills to get a job or get their kin a good job.<br /> <br /> This is why I house rule them -choosing- to be homeless and -choosing- to tend to the lowest. This way, the tribe exist for a reason beyond a dumping ground and the resources restriction (luckily no longer a BAN) makes sense.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://ana-mizuki.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My gallery.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/110-ana-mizuki","name":"Ana Mizuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar110_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2484"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-14T13:48:48-07:00","id":1481116,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ana Mizuki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1480924-bone-gnawers?p=1481054#post1481054\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I actually think the agency part is the main issue. Bone Gnawers by their concept (the omega wolves, poor, homeless, runts of the litter, etc.) lack agency.</div> </div> </div> </div>As much as the status of the Bone Gnawers are socially derived by the rest of the Garou Nation, they are constrained. But everyone has the ability to decide how they are going to respond in that situation. They can't help what happens to them by others, but they can always decide how to react.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ana Mizuki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1480924-bone-gnawers?p=1481054#post1481054\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">While Yellowstone wolf packs (which are WEIRD) have alpha wolves, the omega wolf is entirely a human-created situation.</div> </div> </div> </div>Which is why I also mention &quot;runts of the litter&quot; which is probably more of how the tribe actually started. There would be discarded Garou who simply physically inferior, more mentally challenged, or social misfits that the other tribes didn't want, and didn't pass the qualifications for the Rite of Passage. The rules of character creation and equal build points is an artifact of an RPG making all PCs equal. It doesn't represent the natural bell curve of abilities for NPCs. And these people eventually found a totem that would accept them. Of course, thousands and thousands of years later, there are probably lots of Bone Gnawer stock that would be perfectly acceptable if they were born to other tribes, but because they weren't either they're stuck with the tribe or wouldn't want to leave it.<br /> <br /> The &quot;omega class&quot; of wolves is now primarily a social category enforced by the customs of the Garou Nation.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ana Mizuki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1480924-bone-gnawers?p=1481054#post1481054\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">By their concept, the Gnawers are more of a dumping ground of garou than really a tribe.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes. They have their own internal dynamics, but they still get a lot of their members by accepting cast offs from other tribes, or in the case of Rabble basically performing their rite of passage on any Lost Cub or Ronin they find because they think everyone should have a tribe even if they contribute nothing to the tribe.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ana Mizuki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1480924-bone-gnawers?p=1481054#post1481054\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">However, this makes little sense if you look at the setting as a whole. For one, the Children of Gaia exist to take in those unfortunate.</div> </div> </div> </div>There is certainly some overlap between the Children of Gaia and Bone Gnawers since both are tribes that accept others. But I do see where the Children of Gaia might not accept some Garou. First, the tribe does have some standards of their own. A Garou who is obviously very violently inclined or lacks compassion might not be accepted by Unicorn. This might be why Man Eaters are more prevalent among the Gnawers. I'm sure there's other understandable restrictions we can think of. Then there are those Garou who would reject the Children of Gaia, which in many ways acts like a cult among the Garou. They have fundamentally different values than much of the rest of the Nation and work to inculcate that within their members. There's probably some Garou who could be accepted by the tribe, but choose to leave and accept a rite of passage with the Bone Gnawers instead either because they reject the Children, or ironically have some ambition for leadership and realize they face less competition by entering the Gnawers.<br /> <br /> So I don't have as much problem with this as you may have.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ana Mizuki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1480924-bone-gnawers?p=1481054#post1481054\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Second, as I said in my previous post, most garou are moochers by necessity. There is nothing really stopping a Gnawer from using their skills to get a job or get their kin a good job.</div> </div> </div> </div>Many Garou should be dependent on their kinfolk providing them material items and wealth. However, how much of that may be mooching really depends on what they do in exchange for their kin. Obviously the Garou can provide better physical protection than any police force to their kin. And their Gifts and connections to the spirit world may provide other benefits. If done correctly, the flow of benefits is mutual, and the Garou becomes more akin to a minor feudal lord. It really depends on the amount of exploitation and the benefits they provide. I perceive that style as different from mooching which implies being totally unwanted and provides no reciprocal benefits.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ana Mizuki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1480924-bone-gnawers?p=1481054#post1481054\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is why I house rule them -choosing- to be homeless and -choosing- to tend to the lowest. This way, the tribe exist for a reason beyond a dumping ground and the resources restriction (luckily no longer a BAN) makes sense.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think this works for some of them. And their tribal culture, which should be based around their totem Rat, encourages their protectorate to based on the lowest of society. I just think making it only the homeless is a bit too restrictive. There are plenty of other people who are low on the socioeconomic status and vulnerable, and the Camps tend to play up this angle more and provide some good variety of concepts while remaining true to their theme.<br /> <br /> I perceive the Bone Gnawers to be a tribe that has perhaps the most tensions within it. There are Bone Gnawers who give a damn, those who don't and have dropped out of the fight, and those who are working with the Wyrm in some ways. And even among those who give a damn, there's division in their approach. The Hood may brag about the benefits they provide the poor, but they are still criminals hurting members of the greater community. That is very different than other camps. There's a lot of dysfunction and internal conflict. As an ST, I like that. It provides real conflicts for characters and prevents things from degrading into superheroes because the main conflict often seems black and white.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-14T14:01:40-07:00","id":1481117,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Justycar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1480924-bone-gnawers?p=1481050#post1481050\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If I understood of the book, the more heroic and successful young Bone Gnawers are sometimes adopted by other tribes, so the way is bidirectional, the BG accept the worst of all the tribes and they send back some promises to them.</div> </div> </div> </div>This would make sense. I don't remember that same language, but you could very well be right. It would help if we were given examples or more information. How does this happen? Is this something the tribe supports or resents? What kind of connections do these former members keep with their old tribe, and how long do these connections last (especially between generations)?<br /> <br /> While not stated outright, I remember various statements that seem to imply the Bone Gnawers often don't have a permanent class of kinfolk like the other tribes. They lose a lot over the years, but replenish them through their sexual relations with humans who provide new kin. So it makes sense there is some amount of ferment that see old members leave and new members arrive.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Justycar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1480924-bone-gnawers?p=1481050#post1481050\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There is a merit of 1 point in the page 79 that equates to a point in resources (Struggling), the characters with this merit still maintain miserable jobs and achieve to pay a rent, but most of the tribe lives in the street without this benefit.</div> </div> </div> </div>I remember this merit. I think the merit is fine. But I think the stated mechanics on resources often don't make sense because there probably are a good number of Bone Gnawers who should have the equivalent of Resources 1. I don't think that breaks the tribe. And sometimes, things may happen that would bump that up to 2 or more. Rather than just pretend that is impossible to happen, it's better to explain what happens when it does - do people leave the tribe? do they get mobbed by other members who suck down left over resources so it gets back to 1 or 0? Does Rat demand a sacrifice? These kind of ideas helps the ST. Pretending its impossible and never happens is the equivalent of sticking your head in the sand, and doesn't help the ST or players.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Justycar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1480924-bone-gnawers?p=1481050#post1481050\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">About Rat and other totems, I think that the Rat accepts you as you are. It is said in the book that many BG of the rabble are barely functional werewolves, they live in the rural areas, appear once a month (if they do) in the caern and many have mental illnesses and other problems. But the BG hope that when the moment arrives, they answer. It is a point of conflict, one of the main miseries of the tribe, problems they are handling (page 59).</div> </div> </div> </div>Yep. That has to be for the tribe to work. For me, the idea is that Rat cares about the Swarm, not the individual Garou. The Swarm is everything. So whatever makes the Swarm bigger is good. There is a good and bad side for that approach.<br /> <br /> I think the Rabble was something introduced in the revised tribebook. It's a good concept. I was always dubious of the idea that the Bone Gnawers were the largest tribe, so saying a significant part of the tribe was Rabble cut down their effective numbers. And it is completely up to the ST whether any of them will actually benefit the tribe at all, or if it it just wishful thinking on the people who initiate these members into the tribe.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-14T14:54:08-07:00","id":1481130,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1480924-bone-gnawers?p=1481116#post1481116\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think this works for some of them. And their tribal culture, which should be based around their totem Rat, encourages their protectorate to based on the lowest of society. I just think making it only the homeless is a bit too restrictive. There are plenty of other people who are low on the socioeconomic status and vulnerable, and the Camps tend to play up this angle more and provide some good variety of concepts while remaining true to their theme.</div> </div> </div> </div>At the end of the day, I agree with this. The tribe should have different aspects than just homeless bums. I like the idea of the Rabble, because they serve a good source of garou who do not want to garou but still want safety in numbers.<br /> <br /> As for the Struggling merit? The revised tribebook was pretty gun-ho about the inability of Gnawers to hold onto money. Basically, though Jackal's Blood is not a flaw every Gnawer has, the book treats it as the reason why the Gnawers cannot have money. They are spiritually unable to because their jackal's blood will always result in them splurging and such. So it isn't that the more successful Gnawers leave, it is that they (according to the revised tb) cannot exist at all.<br /> <br /> Which REALLY bothers me as it takes away the agency of the character.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://ana-mizuki.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My gallery.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/110-ana-mizuki","name":"Ana Mizuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar110_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2484"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-14T21:04:23-07:00","id":1481163,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ana Mizuki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1480924-bone-gnawers?p=1481130#post1481130\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As for the Struggling merit? The revised tribebook was pretty gun-ho about the inability of Gnawers to hold onto money. Basically, though Jackal's Blood is not a flaw every Gnawer has, the book treats it as the reason why the Gnawers cannot have money. They are spiritually unable to because their jackal's blood will always result in them splurging and such. So it isn't that the more successful Gnawers leave, it is that they (according to the revised tb) cannot exist at all.<br /> <br /> Which REALLY bothers me as it takes away the agency of the character.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, I agree. I think they were trying to figure out a way to not have a billionaire Bone Gnawer Garou or kinfolk who made good and now supports the tribe, but went about it a bad way.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-16T03:12:20-07:00","id":1481219,"title":"","content":"I find it okay. If it was up to the players to portray the BGs that they could not handle money (or what ever the humans thing of Resources) then inevitably the special snowflake would arise. That might be fine in a table top game, if all players are okay with the special snowflake not adhering to what some might see as a core theme of the tribe. It is less cool in a 40+ players 20 year running LARP game, for if one player has it another player wants it and several other players find it wrong and so on....<br /> <br /> Making it a metaphysical thing and get on with it might seem quick and dirty, but it is hard to argue it, therefore it sticks.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-16T04:01:00-07:00","id":1481225,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>heinrich</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1480924-bone-gnawers?p=1481219#post1481219\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I find it okay. If it was up to the players to portray the BGs that they could not handle money (or what ever the humans thing of Resources) then inevitably the special snowflake would arise. That might be fine in a table top game, if all players are okay with the special snowflake not adhering to what some might see as a core theme of the tribe. It is less cool in a 40+ players 20 year running LARP game, for if one player has it another player wants it and several other players find it wrong and so on....<br /> <br /> Making it a metaphysical thing and get on with it might seem quick and dirty, but it is hard to argue it, therefore it sticks.</div> </div> </div> </div>If that is such a big problem for you, why isn't it caught in the character creation?<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://ana-mizuki.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My gallery.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/110-ana-mizuki","name":"Ana Mizuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar110_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2484"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-16T04:37:39-07:00","id":1481227,"title":"","content":"One could catch it in character creation - I mean we do, since the Background is prohibited.<br /> Doesn't prevent them from acquiring wealth in-game.<ul><li>Inheritance by Glass Walker with Resources, for some obscure reason.</li> <li>Manipulating the stock market.</li> <li>Open Seal on the armoured car service.</li> <li>Manipulation the lottery.</li> <li>Even taking the possessions of slain fomori can make some Bone Gnawer rich....</li> </ul><br /> Backgrounds are subject to change in game, after all....<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-16T04:58:24-07:00","id":1481229,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>heinrich</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1480924-bone-gnawers?p=1481227#post1481227\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">One could catch it in character creation - I mean we do, since the Background is prohibited.<br /> Doesn't prevent them from acquiring wealth in-game.<ul><li>Inheritance by Glass Walker with Resources, for some obscure reason.</li> <li>Manipulating the stock market.</li> <li>Open Seal on the armoured car service.</li> <li>Manipulation the lottery.</li> <li>Even taking the possessions of slain fomori can make some Bone Gnawer rich....</li> </ul><br /> <br /> Backgrounds are subject to change in game, after all....</div> </div> </div> </div>If it is in-game, they have had to earn it through roleplay, so I don't see why a character like that would be a snowflake. We had a Gnawer theurge hit 4 in resources in one game, because she managed basically half a town's resources. Because no one else would.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://ana-mizuki.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My gallery.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/110-ana-mizuki","name":"Ana Mizuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar110_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2484"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-16T06:26:42-07:00","id":1481233,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ana Mizuki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1480924-bone-gnawers?p=1481229#post1481229\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If it is in-game, they have had to earn it through roleplay, so I don't see why a character like that would be a snowflake. We had a Gnawer theurge hit 4 in resources in one game, because she managed basically half a town's resources. Because no one else would.</div> </div> </div> </div>Managing someone else's Resources isn't the issue. Neither is managing resources at all - Bone Gnawer manage their septs and packs and get by with what they have and often provide of those even less fortunate, so managing isn't the problem when it comes to their capabilities.<br /> <br /> It is, that they don't achieve or keep for extended periods of time, what other would count as personal wealth. That is a difference.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-16T08:18:27-07:00","id":1481238,"title":"","content":"Black Fox, I have been looking for a concrete example of a successful BG adopted by other tribe, but I have not found it. So, it could be entirely possible that I unconsciously have brought the idea to give coherence to the text. In the end, I understand from the text that the BG actively avoid the &quot;trappings&quot; of the money and the system to avoid being tainted by the Weaver (unlike the GW). This is said in the first chapter (page 15).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24202-justycar","name":"Justycar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24202_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"320"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-16T10:53:27-07:00","id":1481248,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>heinrich</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1480924-bone-gnawers?p=1481233#post1481233\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Managing someone else's Resources isn't the issue. Neither is managing resources at all - Bone Gnawer manage their septs and packs and get by with what they have and often provide of those even less fortunate, so managing isn't the problem when it comes to their capabilities.<br /> <br /> It is, that they don't achieve or keep for extended periods of time, what other would count as personal wealth. That is a difference.</div> </div> </div> </div>It was her resources, because they were in her name.<br /> <br /> But that besides the point, earning money through roleplay is no more snowflake than a reluctant garou cub becoming a respected elder through play. PCs change over the course of the game, be it LARP or TT or chat.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://ana-mizuki.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My gallery.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/110-ana-mizuki","name":"Ana Mizuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar110_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2484"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-16T11:15:59-07:00","id":1481250,"title":"","content":"Becoming a wealthy Bone Gnawer is special, since it is an exception and not inline with the theme of the tribe, I'd say, while becoming a wealthy Glass Walker is not, for it is not against the theme of the tribe.<br /> <br /> Doesn't matter if a character specialness is starting from creation or acquired in game if it is being different from the theme of the tribe just for the sake of being different.<br /> Sure, characters evolve during the course of the game, but should they be able to overcome defining elements of their tribe? Well, there is the possibility to leave the tribe, be adopted into another or become Ronin.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-16T12:39:36-07:00","id":1481253,"title":"","content":"I think that's the heart of what we are talking about here. I think everyone agrees that a wealthy or too prosperous Bone Gnawer goes against the theme of the tribe. The question is what to do about it?<br /> <br /> One solution is to just allow it. But that's not how the sourcebooks handle it, and I don't hear anyone on this thread arguing that. I think we all agree the Billionaire Bone Gnawer is not a good concept.<br /> <br /> Another solution is to just forbid it by fiat. This is how the sourcebooks try to do it. That Background is not allowed. If a character does get money, it inexplicably gets spent and lost - no explanation. The ST says no. That may work as a game mechanic, but it doesn't work in the setting itself. The suspension of disbelief requires the setting to work on its own. In real life, there's no such thing as an ST who simply stops things. People have agency. This is why I dislike how the sourcebooks handle this issue.<br /> <br /> The other solution is to try to determine realistic reasons in game. &quot;Bone Gnawer culture discourages that&quot; is a fine <i>starting</i> point, but it doesn't explain every scenario. Bone Gnawers aren't known to be scrupulous about obeying the rules anyway. And there will always be someone who somehow gains riches - whether that's a real fortune, or just achieving a solid middle class lifestyle. What happens then?<br /> <br /> Ultimately there needs to be both a carrot and stick. <br /> <br /> We have mostly talked about the carrot. The carrot is that Bone Gnawer culture makes it impossible to accumulate wealth past a certain point, probably Resources 1 with Resources 0 being the norm. What is it about the culture that does that? My current idea is that Rat emphasizes the survival of the Swarm over the well being of any individual member. (If you have other ideas, that's fine too.) It was originally a survival tactic - Bone Gnawers share everything they have with one another. That worked well when everyone was a hunter-gatherer or subsistence farmer. It works less well in a modern economy where people are expected to save money for the future. <br /> <br /> Any Bone Gnawer who begins to do well for himself simply gets approached by the closest Bone Gnawers around him and is expected to share the wealth. Maybe that's allowing the local sept to sleep in your apartment because who have a free couch or extra room. Maybe that is taking everyone out on your dime to the local diner. Or spending your money to hand out free groceries to everyone. That would effectively prevent anyone from ever accumulating wealth. It just doesn't fritter away because every Bone Gnawer is incapable of handling money. It's because anything over a certain point gets handed over to someone else. An individual or single family that has $1000 saved away has the beginning of a nice bank account that can help them get past living from paycheck to paycheck. But if that $1000 gets divided between 10 Bone Gnawers and their collective 100 kinfolk, then everybody just has less than $10 - which is nothing. <br /> <br /> What is some lucky Bone Gnawer or their kinfolk gets a lottery ticket and wins Power Ball and gets $100 million? Well, you get more than just the local Bone Gnawers coming to you then. They hear about it at other septs and come calling. After all, it is the Bone Gnawer way to share your good fortunes. That Power Ball winner at the Sept of the Green in Central Park finds that the Bone Gnawers from the Sept of the Awakening in DC and most of the Hillfolk in Appalachia come by to get their share. The Rat Finks could use some new laptops. The Hood takes their share so they can share with the unfortunate. Frankweilers could use some donations to help their education efforts. And so on. And pretty soon, it's all gone with little to show for it for any one individual Bone Gnawer.<br /> <br /> It's easy to piss away even a lot of money and have nothing to show for it. But I find the above a more believable way it can happen then a blanket statement than all Bone Gnawers, as individuals, can't handle money. Maybe others feel differently.<br /> <br /> But that can only go so far. Because that Bone Gnawer or the Bone Gnawer kin can always say &quot;No&quot; and defy the tribal culture.<br /> <br /> That's where the stick comes in. And the stick is either implied threat and intimidation to get them to share the wealth, or expulsion/exile/flight from the tribe. The first is easy to imagine. Bone Gnawers are still Garou and can be dangerous. Peer pressure, innuendo, or outright threats can probably enforce most of the culture. So would various Punishment Rites help enforce the culture. Too &quot;greedy&quot;? You get hit with Rite of Ostracism or the Stone of Scorn. <br /> <br /> Sure, you lost that $1000 in your savings account, but your <i>real</i> safety net is all the other Bone Gnawers and their kin who will help you when you truly need it. That $1000 didn't mean anything. It was just an illusion. That was never really going to help you. One car accident or hospital trip would wipe that out. It's better to rely on the tribe and your kin. Better now that you didn't give into greed and keep that to yourself. So the tribal culture has disincentives against people trying to better their condition past a certain point.<br /> <br /> But there will still be those who end up rejecting that and somehow prove able to withstand the pressure. Perhaps the Bone Gnawer kinfolk family flees elsewhere and starts anew. The Bone Gnawer Garou is part of a multi-tribal pack who supports her against her tribe. The local Glass Walkers or Children of Gaia (or whoever( is willing to take them in and perform the Rite of Renunciation and then Adoption/Acceptance. In any case, they get away. If the Bone Gnawers don't know about it, they just wonder whatever happened to the Palmer family and move on with their lives. But in cases where the tribe was defied, that Garou or kinfolk family has become traitors whose greed and selfishness overwhelmed them and caused them to betray Rat and the tribe. <br /> <br /> Now, not all Bone Gnawers may be like that. Some Gnawers might feel it's a good thing if people are able to improve themselves and move on (particularly for kinfolk). I imagine the Frankweilers would be supportive. Since the Bone Gnawers overall aren't very organized and let each other do what they want, lots can escape notice. Bone Gnawers may care about the individual kinfolk they are connected to, but they're not like other tribes in obsessing over their lineage. Lots of kin are probably left to their own devices and can be adopted by the Glass Walkers or someone else.<br /> <br /> But some Bone Gnawers would be fierce about such a &quot;betrayal&quot; because it is against the way of Rat. Maybe less about kinfolk, but definitely if it was a Garou. So there can be tension here if the ST wants. Some septs and packs might not care much. Either bon voyage or good riddance. Other septs and packs might be very hostile - maybe perform the Rite of the Lone Wolf, or even the Hunt, or proclaim the person an enemy and let her know if they ever find her on their territory they'll kill her. This provides new tools for an ST to tell a story. Conflicts are good for the game. You can explain why the local Glass Walkers and Bone Gnawers hate each other in this city. Or give some NPCs an unusual back story (perhaps with an appropriate Merit or Flaw). Or provide a moral dilemma for the PCs. Yet every character has agency, and the theme of the tribes remain the same.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}